Betrayed and Abandoned!
What was foretold and fulfilled regarding the Messiah’s betrayal?
David prophesied: “The man at peace with me, in whom I trusted, who was eating my bread, has magnified his heel against me. ”
A person who ate bread with someone was thought to be his friend.
So the betrayal of Jesus by Judas Iscariot was treachery of the worst sort.
Jesus called attention to the fulfillment of David’s prophetic words when He referred to his betrayer and told his apostles: “I am not talking about all of you; I know the ones I have chosen. But it is in order that the Scripture might be fulfilled, ‘ He that used to feed on my bread has lifted up his heel against me. ’ ” — John 13: 18.
The Messiah’s betrayer would receive 30 pieces of silver — the price of a slave! Drawing on Zechariah 11: 12, 13, Matthew showed that Jesus was betrayed for such a paltry sum.
But why did Matthew say that this had been foretold “through Jeremiah the prophet ”?
In Matthew’s day, Jeremiah may have been placed first in a group of Bible books that included Zechariah. ( Compare Luke 24: 44.)
On Nisan 14, 33 C.E., Jesus told his disciples: “All of you will be stumbled in connection with me on this night, for it is written, ‘ I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered about. ’ ”
The Messiah would be tried and condemned.
False witnesses would testify against the Messiah. “ Violent witnesses rise up, ” said the psalmist David.
True to prophecy, “the chief priests and the entire Sanhedrin were looking for false witness against Jesus in order to put him to death. ”
Lying testimony mattered little to Jesus ’ rabid enemies, who sought his death.
The Messiah would be silent before his accusers.
Isaiah prophesied: “He was hard pressed, and he was letting himself be afflicted; yet he would not open his mouth. He was being brought just like a sheep to the slaughtering; and like a ewe that before her shearers has become mute, he also would not open his mouth. ”
Nevertheless, Jesus “did not answer him, no, not a word, so that the governor wondered very much. ”
Confirming the fulfillment of these prophecies, the Gospel writer Mark said: “Some started to spit on and to cover his whole face and hit him with their fists and say to him: ‘ Prophesy! ’
And, slapping him in the face, the court attendants took him. ” Mark states that soldiers “would hit him on the head with a reed and spit upon him and, bending their knees , they would do obeisance to him. ”
Of course, Jesus did nothing to provoke such abuse.
