Many have simply grown tired of being told that the end is near. But could those be reactions to fiction rather than to fact?
You might be surprised to learn what the Bible really says about the end of the world.
Far from planning to destroy it, he promises to “bring to ruin those ruining the earth ” — and to protect it from permanent damage. — Revelation 11: 18.
That Bible verse says: “The heavens and the earth that are now are stored up for fire. ” Does this not show that the earth will be burned up?
For example, when Genesis 11: 1 says: “All the earth continued to be of one language, ” it uses“ earth ” to mean human society.
Instead, the Flood wiped out a violent society, or “earth. ”
Such a positive view of “the end of the world ” is implied by the Bible translations that render this expression“ the conclusion of the system of things ” or “the end of the age. ”
Jesus called that future period “the renewal of all things. ” At that time, he will restore humanity to the conditions that God originally intended.
If we do “the will of God, ” what he asks of us, we need not fear the end of the world.
Instead, we can look forward to it.
In the book The Last Days Are Here Again, Professor Richard Kyle writes that “sudden change and social chaos create an atmosphere conducive to predictions of the end of the world. ”
THE BIBLE’S ANSWER: “Whenever it is that they are saying: ‘ Peace and security! ’
then sudden destruction is to be instantly upon them just as the pang of distress upon a pregnant woman; and they will by no means escape. ” — 1 THESSALONIANS 5: 3.
Her doctor might estimate the birth date; yet, even if the event should delay, she would still be sure that her baby will soon be born.
Rather than acknowledge the fundamental changes during the last days, they would scoff: “From the day our forefathers fell asleep in death, all things are continuing exactly as from creation’s beginning. ”
Why, then, do we continue to highlight the nearness of the end?
Consider this example: A lookout in a fire tower might see what he thinks is a wisp of smoke on the horizon and sound what proves to be a false alarm. Later, though, his alertness could save lives.
DRAW CLOSE TO GOD
Jehovah, the “Lord of heaven and earth, ” may hide or reveal the truth according to his sovereign will. But God is not arbitrary in this matter of revelation.
On what basis does he reveal Bible truth to some and not to others? Jehovah favors the humble, not the haughty.
We come back to the question raised at the outset: Do you want to know the truth about God? If so, you may find comfort in knowing that God does not favor those who consider themselves worldly - wise.
