What is the divine formula for acquiring peace of mind? What principle can help us to serve God without regrets?
Do you wish that you could serve God without regrets — at least from this day forward?
Is there a true - life example from whom we can learn to do that?
There certainly is — the apostle Paul.
As a young Pharisee, Paul did things that he later regretted.
For example, he led a campaign of vicious persecution against Christ’s disciples. The Bible record states that immediately after the martyring of Stephen, “Saul ... began to deal outrageously with the congregation.
Invading one house after another and, dragging out both men and women, he would turn them over to prison. ”
Explain how Saul was converted from persecuting Jesus ’ followers to preaching about Christ.
It was Saul’s intent to go to Damascus, tear Jesus ’ disciples from their homes, and drag them to Jerusalem to face the wrath of the Sanhedrin. However, he failed because he was in conflict with the Head of the Christian congregation.
While Saul was on his way to Damascus, Jesus confronted him, and Saul was blinded by a miraculous light. Then Jesus sent Saul on to Damascus to wait for further contact.
What shows that Paul was well - aware of his painful past?
Later, he again mentioned his regrettable past when writing to the Corinthians, to the Philippians, and to Timothy.
Farrar added that it is only when we weigh the terrible significance of this sad period in Paul’s life that “we feel the load of remorse which must have lain upon him, and the taunts to which he was liable from malignant enemies. ” At times, Paul was perhaps approached by brothers in the various congregations he visited, brothers meeting him for the first time, saying, ‘ So you’re Paul — you’re the one who persecuted us! ’ — Acts 9: 21.
Paul deeply appreciated the merciful way in which he was treated, and he wanted to make sure that God’s undeserved kindness had not been extended to him in vain. So he “labored in excess ” of all the other apostles.
Yes, Paul learned the principle of how to serve without amassing more regrets.
Is that something that you have learned?
Or it may be that you have unpleasant feelings of regret for some other reason.
The question is, What can you do about it? Many people worry!
To worry continually means to plague oneself, vex oneself, persecute oneself.
It causes great anxiety.
Imagine trying to move forward by rocking for hours in a rocking chair, expending all that energy but getting nowhere!
Some have the tendency to let past failings overwhelm them to the point that they feel that they are unworthy in God’s eyes. They may feel beyond the reach of God’s mercy because they strayed off course either so far or so often.
We all must follow the divine formula for acquiring peace of mind: “He that is covering over his transgressions will not succeed, but he that is confessing and leaving them will be shown mercy. ”
Thus, we can confess to God, pray for his forgiveness, and take steps to right the wrong. If we have done these things, then we can enjoy the mercy of the One who ‘ forgives in a large way. ’ — Isa.
Only a confession to God.
So instead of worrying about the past, we should be concerned about the present and the future.
What are we doing right now, or failing to do, that years from now we will wish we had not done or had done differently?
As the great tribulation nears, we do not want to be plagued with such anxious thoughts as these: ‘ Could I have done more in God’s service?
Why didn’t I pioneer when I had the opportunity?
What prevented me from reaching out to serve as a ministerial servant?
Did I make a real effort to put on the new personality?
Am I the sort of person Jehovah wants in his new world? ’
Instead of just worrying about such sobering questions, we want to use them to analyze ourselves and make sure that we are giving our best in Jehovah’s service.
Otherwise, we could be continuing in a way of life that may result in even more regrets. — 2 Tim.
Not at all!
He will not forget your life of self - sacrifice.
What principle did Paul learn that helped him serve God without having more regrets?
Phillips translation, Paul wrote: “I leave the past behind and with hands outstretched to whatever lies ahead I go straight for the goal. ”
All of us can apply the principle behind Paul’s words.
No, we may not literally forget past mistakes, but we need not constantly berate ourselves for them.
The repeated reference to women also being the object of Saul’s persecution shows that they played a large part in the spread of Christianity in the first century, just as they do today. — Ps.
In what ways do Christian elders contribute to the joy of their fellow believers?
How might all of us add to the spirit of joy in the congregation?
What caused Paul to rejoice over the Christians in Corinth?
The apostle Paul was in the harbor city of Troas, but he could not stop thinking about Corinth.
He had also sent his fellow worker Titus to them and had arranged for Titus to report back to him in Troas. Now Paul was waiting for Titus in Troas, longing to hear how the Corinthians were faring.
He told them: “Not that we are the masters over your faith, but we are fellow workers for your joy, for it is by your faith that you are standing. ” What did Paul mean by that?
And how should those words affect Christian elders today?
Hence, Paul saw no need to control the faith of his brothers, and he had no desire to do so.
Today, elders follow Paul’s example by expressing confidence in their brothers ’ faith and motives for serving God.
Rather than making rigid rules for the congregation, elders rely on Scriptural principles and direction from Jehovah’s organization.
Paul also stated: “We are fellow workers for your joy. ”
Moreover, whenever Paul used the term “fellow workers ” in his letters, it always referred to his close associates, such as Apollos, Aquila, Prisca, Timothy, Titus, and others. Hence, by saying: “We are fellow workers for your joy, ” Paul assured the Corinthians that he and his companions wanted to do all they could to promote the joy of all members of the congregation.
We will consider the responses to what question, and on what should we reflect?
Recently, a group of zealous brothers and sisters living in different parts of the world were invited to respond to the question, “What words and deeds of an elder have added to your joy? ”
As we now consider the observations made by that group of fellow believers, see how their comments compare with how you might have responded.
Each of these servants of Jehovah showed sincere interest in another person by using the individual’s name.
Elders will particularly add to their brothers ’ joy when they remember their names as they call on them for comments during the Watchtower Study or other meetings. — Compare John 10: 3.
What was one important way in which Paul followed the example set by Jehovah and Jesus?
Thus, in the same letter in which he stated his desire to work for his brothers ’ joy, Paul wrote: “I have great boasting in regard to you. ”
Paul expressed similar sentiments to other congregations. In fact, after he mentioned Persis in his letter to the congregation in Rome, Paul added: “She performed many labors in the Lord. ”
Why does giving and receiving commendation add to a spirit of joy in the congregation?
Elders today also see the importance of putting their feelings of appreciation for their brothers into words.
And fellow believers definitely need to hear the elders ’ reassuring words. A sister in her mid - 50 ’ s spoke for many when she said: “At work I seldom get a compliment.
It makes me feel loved by my heavenly Father. ”
He says: “The elder’s words gave me a real boost! ” Indeed, by sincerely commending fellow believers, an elder lifts their spirit and deepens their joy.
To give such personal encouragement, they visit their brothers and sisters in their homes if circumstances allow. During such shepherding visits, they want to “impart some spiritual gift ” to them.
What are the person’s challenges?
What thoughts could build him or her up? What scripture or experience of a Bible character would be applicable to his or her circumstances?
During shepherding visits, an elder allows his brothers and sisters to express themselves while he listens attentively.
Who in the congregation are in need of encouragement, and why?
Who will benefit from receiving shepherding visits?
Not because David had lost his courage but because he had lost his strength. The record states: “David grew tired. ”
As soon as Ishbi - benob noticed David’s moment of physical weakness, he “got to think of striking David down. ”
But then, just before the giant thrust his weapon into David, “at once, Abishai the son of Zeruiah came to his help and struck the Philistine down and put him to death. ”
How grateful David must have been that Abishai had kept an eye on him and had quickly come to his aid when his life was in danger! What lessons can we draw from this account?
At such moments, the timely support given by an elder can help us to regain our joy and our strength, as many have experienced. A pioneer in her mid - 60 ’ s related: “Some time ago, I did not feel well, and field service tired me out.
At times, elders spend sleepless nights in prayerful concern for God’s flock or in giving spiritual support to fellow believers.
Indeed, out of love for his brothers, Paul fully spent himself in strengthening them.
Moreover, we are grateful that they stand ready to come to our aid at moments when we feel overwhelmed by the pressures of this world.
