Average Regular Pioneers: 367
Translation: 14 languages
On the average, each publisher welcomed about six interested people to the Memorial
Why must we avoid bad associations?
How can we support theocratic arrangements?
What will help us to put spiritual things first and preserve our Christian identity?
RESIDENTS of Jerusalem are talking. A notorious foreigner is staying in a temple chamber.
How did Israel become a sanctified nation?
So God sanctified them, or set them apart as his chosen people. What a privilege that was!
We will consider what factors that can help us to remain a sanctified people?
Like the Israelites, Witnesses of Jehovah today have been sanctified by God in a general sense. Both anointed Christians and those of the “great crowd ” are holy, set apart for sacred service.
What can help us to prevent this from happening so that we can remain holy and useful in Jehovah’s service? In this study, we will consider four factors highlighted in Nehemiah chapter 13: (1) Avoid bad associations; (2) support theocratic arrangements; (3) put spiritual things first; and (4) preserve your Christian identity.
Who were Eliashib and Tobiah, and why might Eliashib have been associating with Tobiah? Read Nehemiah 13: 4 - 9.
So why would the high priest provide space in a temple dining hall for such a man as Tobiah?
Tobiah had become a close associate of Eliashib. Tobiah and his son Jehohanan had married Jewish women, and many Jews spoke highly of Tobiah.
One of Eliashib’s grandsons was married to the daughter of Sanballat, governor of Samaria, who was one of Tobiah’s closest associates. These ties may explain why High Priest Eliashib allowed an unbeliever and opposer to influence him.
But Nehemiah showed loyalty to Jehovah by throwing all of Tobiah’s furniture outside the dining hall. How do elders and others avoid defiling their sanctified position before Jehovah?
Unless we adhere to his righteous standards, we will not remain sanctified before him.
Christian elders are guided by Jehovah’s thinking, not by their own opinions or feelings.
What should all dedicated servants of Jehovah remember when it comes to their associations?
Eliashib had set a good example for the people by fully supporting Nehemiah in the rebuilding of Jerusalem’s walls. In time, however, the unwholesome influence of Tobiah and others apparently led Eliashib to do things that left him defiled before Jehovah.
Not having this support, the Levites were abandoning their assignments and going off to work in their fields.
So Nehemiah took steps to have the tithes collected. He appointed trustworthy men to oversee the temple stores and future distributions.
What privilege do God’s people have in supporting true worship?
Is there a lesson here for us?
Yes, for we are reminded that we have the privilege of honoring Jehovah with our valuable things.
When we make contributions to support his work, we are merely giving to Jehovah what already belongs to him.
We may not think that we have much to give, but if the desire is there, all of us can have a share. — 2 Cor.
The encouraging words and experiences of the couple helped to motivate the children to make spiritual progress.
What fine example is being set by appointed men in the congregation?
What can we learn from Nehemiah’s example?
He added: “The man that has entered into God’s rest has also himself rested from his own works, just as God did from his own. ” As Christians, we can enter into God’s rest by obediently working in harmony with his advancing purpose.
How was the very identity of the nation as a sanctified people being threatened in Nehemiah’s day?
In Nehemiah’s day, Israelite men were marrying foreign women. During his first visit to Jerusalem, Nehemiah had all the older men sign a written agreement pledging that they would not marry pagans.
A few years later, however, he found that not only had Jewish men taken foreign wives but they were about to lose their very identity as God’s sanctified people!
The children of these foreign women could not read or speak Hebrew. When they got older, would they identify themselves as Israelites?
Or would they rather think of themselves as Ashdodites, Ammonites, or Moabites?
Without knowing the Hebrew language, was it likely that they would be able to understand God’s Law?
How could they get to know Jehovah and choose to serve him instead of the false gods their mothers worshipped?
Swift and decisive action was needed, and Nehemiah took such action. — Neh.
How can parents help their children to have a personal relationship with Jehovah?
Today, we need to take positive action in order to help our children acquire a Christian identity. Parents, ask yourselves, ‘ How well do my children speak the “pure language ” of Scriptural truth?
It takes time to learn a language, especially when we are surrounded by distractions.
This includes setting a proper example, specifying clear boundaries, and discussing with your children the consequences of decisions.
How can we be remembered by Jehovah “for good ”?
God will never forget those who have reverential fear of him and love for his name. — Heb.
Like Nehemiah, our names will be in God’s book of remembrance if we continue to avoid bad associations, support theocratic arrangements, give spiritual matters priority, and safeguard our Christian identity.
Let us ‘ keep testing whether we are in the faith. ’ If we maintain our sanctified relationship with Jehovah, he will remember us “for good. ”
What might cause us to become “enraged against Jehovah ”?
What are five factors that can help us to avoid becoming enraged against God?
We should remember what when faced with difficult problems?
Why should we not blame Jehovah for mankind’s problems?
Would you blurt out, “Why did my wife do this? ”
Or would you be more likely to ask, “Who did this? ”
Why? Because you know that your cherished mate would not be the cause of such a wanton act of vandalism.
Our study of the Scriptures has helped us to identify the real cause of many of the world’s difficulties.
However, we cannot blame Satan for all our woes. Why not?
Because some of our problems are the result of our own mistakes.
Even though we might admit that fact, our imperfect nature can distort our thinking and cause us to go down a path that eventually leads to disaster.
Instead of blaming ourselves or Satan for a problem, we could start to blame Jehovah.
How could a Christian become “enraged against Jehovah ”? Is it really possible that we could become “enraged against Jehovah ”?
Surely to do so would be futile. What would we hope to gain?
What could prompt us to be “enraged against Jehovah ”?
How can we avoid that trap?
It is vital that we know the answers to these questions. Why, our very relationship with Jehovah God is involved!
Why did the Israelites in Moses ’ time start to complain about Jehovah?
What could cause the heart of a faithful servant of Jehovah to start to complain about his God?
Let us consider five factors and analyze Bible examples that highlight how some in the past fell into this trap. — 1 Cor.
The Israelites had just been delivered from slavery in Egypt. Jehovah had miraculously brought ten plagues on that oppressive nation and thereafter destroyed Pharaoh and his military force in the Red Sea.
God’s people were poised to enter the Promised Land. Yet, at that crucial moment, the Israelites started to complain about Jehovah.
What resulted? A whole generation was not allowed to enter into that “good land. ”
What caused God’s people in Isaiah’s day to start to blame Jehovah for their situation? Hardships and difficulties may discourage us.
In Isaiah’s day, the nation of Judah found themselves in dire straits. They were surrounded by enemies.
Because of not having all the facts, the Israelites in Ezekiel’s day felt that Jehovah’s way was “not adjusted right. ” It was as if they had set themselves up as judges of God, putting their own standards of justice above Jehovah’s and judging him based on their own limited understanding of events.
We shift the blame for our own sins and mistakes.
Right at the beginning of human history, Adam blamed God for his own sin.
What lesson can we learn from Jonah?
Could we likewise become so self - centered that we become “enraged against Jehovah ” for not bringing the end sooner? If we have been preaching for decades that Jehovah’s day is close, could we start to become impatient with Jehovah when others criticize us for proclaiming what the Bible says? — 2 Pet.
If in our heart we start to question some of Jehovah’s activities, what must we not neglect?
What can we do if our sinful heart starts to question some of Jehovah’s activities?
What will help us not to be affected by the negative speech of others?
Do not allow negative speech to affect you. The Israelites in Moses ’ day had ample reason to believe that Jehovah would successfully usher them into the Promised Land.
But when faced with the negative report of the ten unfaithful spies, they did not “remember his hand. ” If we meditate on Jehovah’s activities, remembering all the good things he has done for us, we will strengthen our relationship with him.
Under such circumstances, our relationship with Jehovah can be affected. When the Israelites questioned Aaron’s appointment and position, Jehovah viewed that action as murmuring against Him.
Similarly, if we were to start grumbling and murmuring about those whom Jehovah is using to direct the earthly part of his organization, we could by inference be complaining about Jehovah. — Heb.
What do we need to remember when we are having problems?
Remember that Jehovah does not cause our problems. Though the Israelites in Isaiah’s day had turned away from Jehovah, He still wanted to help them.
No matter what problem we may face, we can find comfort in knowing that Jehovah cares for us and wants to help us.
In fact, he promises to provide us with the strength needed to keep on enduring. — 1 Cor.
If we are suffering some form of injustice — even as the faithful man Job did — we need to remind ourselves that Jehovah is not the cause of the unfairness.
May we, like Job’s friend Elihu, acknowledge: “Far be it from the true God to act wickedly, and the Almighty to act unjustly! ”
Rather than causing our problems, Jehovah gives us “every good gift and every perfect present. ” — Jas.
Why should we never doubt Jehovah?
God is perfect, and his thoughts are higher than ours.
No doubt, you have experienced the truthfulness of this proverb: “The first to present his case seems right, till another comes forward and questions him. ” — Prov.
If we have a trusted friend who does something that we might not at first understand or that we feel is unusual, will we be quick to accuse him of some wrongdoing? Or will we be inclined to give our friend the benefit of the doubt, especially if we have known that person for many years?
If we deal with our imperfect friends in such a loving manner, how much more should we be inclined to trust our heavenly Father, whose ways and thoughts are much higher than ours!
Why is it important for us to lay the blame where it belongs? Lay the blame where it belongs.
Well, we may be responsible for some of our problems. If we are, we need to acknowledge that fact.
Imagine that a driver greatly exceeds the recommended speed limit when traveling around a sharp curve and he crashes.
No, of course not!
Similarly, Jehovah has created us with free will. But he has also provided us with guidelines on how to make wise decisions.
Of course, not all our problems are a result of our personal mistakes and wrong actions.
What should we remember if we become discouraged because of our problems?
When you are undergoing hardship and difficulties, remember the example of Joshua and Caleb. Unlike the ten other spies, these two faithful men brought back a positive report.
Were they blessed? Absolutely!
Whereas a whole generation died in the wilderness, both of these men eventually entered the Promised Land.
Similarly, we will receive Jehovah’s blessing if we do not “tire out ” in doing his will. — Gal.
If you are discouraged because of problems, the imperfections of others, or your own imperfections, what should you do?
Always remain close to him, and never allow your heart to become enraged against him!
What does it mean to “consider one another ”?
How can we ‘ incite one another to love and fine works ’?
How can we ‘ encourage one another ’?
What helped 230 of Jehovah’s Witnesses to survive the death march at the end of World War II?
Along with God - given “power beyond what is normal, ” their love for one another helped them survive the ordeal. — 2 Cor.
Today, we are not on such a death march, but we do face many challenges.
After the establishment of God’s Kingdom in 1914, Satan was ousted from heaven and confined to the vicinity of the earth, having “great anger, knowing he has a short period of time. ”
As this world approaches Armageddon, Satan is using trials and pressures in an attempt to weaken us spiritually. Added to this are the stresses of day - to - day life.
They need to view us as their friends and be assured of our love for them.
Concerning a repentant wrongdoer in Corinth, Paul wrote: “You should kindly forgive and comfort him, that somehow such a man may not be swallowed up by his being overly sad.
According to one lexicon, the term rendered “confirm ” means to“ ratify, validate, make legally binding. ” We simply cannot assume that the person understands our love and concern for him.
What does it mean to “incite to love and fine works ”? “ Let us consider one another to incite to love and fine works, ” wrote Paul.
My own experience has taught me how important it is to give encouragement to others, and I go out of my way to give it. ” Commendation can stimulate all — including pioneers, elderly ones, and those who may be discouraged. — Rom.
When ‘ those who have spiritual qualifications try to readjust a man who has taken a false step, ’ loving counsel and appropriate commendation may motivate the wrongdoer to return to the course of fine works.
When an elder reminded her of her past faithful service, her emotions were stirred. She allowed the elders an opportunity to reassure her of Jehovah’s love.
What can be said about using shame, criticism, or guilt to motivate others?
Moreover, let us “be swift about hearing ” and“ slow about speaking. ” If we listen empathetically, we may be able to identify situations that discourage a fellow Christian and say something to help him deal with those circumstances.
A discouraged person may not immediately feel better or respond quickly to the help we offer.
In the past, Jehovah dealt patiently with those of his servants who at times were discouraged. For instance, God was very gracious with Elijah, considering his feelings.
Jehovah provided what the prophet needed to carry on in his service. Because David was genuinely repentant, Jehovah kindly forgave him.
God also helped the writer of Psalm 73, who almost gave up serving Him. Jehovah is gracious and kind to us, especially when we are downhearted and discouraged.
His mercies are “new each morning, ” and they“ will certainly not come to an end. ”
Jehovah expects us to follow his example and treat the depressed ones with tenderness.
As the end of this system draws near, what must we be determined to do, and why?
Soon, all of Jehovah’s worshippers will unitedly walk into the new world of righteousness. May we be determined to help one another along the path that leads to everlasting life.
How does Satan deceive people? What can you do to protect your relationship with Jehovah?
IT IS not unusual to care about what others think of us.
Emphasizing the ‘ sort of persons we ought to be ’ from Jehovah’s viewpoint, the apostle Peter urges us to engage in “holy acts of conduct and deeds of godly devotion. ”
Satan the Devil does not want us to seek God’s approval.
In fact, he does all he can to get us to abandon our relationship with Jehovah. Satan does not hesitate to use lies and deceit to seduce us and draw us away from the God we worship.
What can I do to protect my relationship with Jehovah? ’
In an attempt to break our attachment to God, Satan targets the source of our desires — our heart.
Satan’s weapons include “the things in the world. ”
How did Satan use “the desire of the flesh ” to tempt Eve? Humans have a fundamental requirement for sustaining the body — the need for food.
Using “the desire of the flesh, ” how did Satan try to entice Jesus, and why did this temptation not succeed? Satan used the same tactic when he tried to tempt Jesus in the wilderness.
After Jesus had fasted for 40 days and 40 nights, Satan attempted to appeal to Jesus ’ desire for food. “ If you are a son of God, ” said Satan, “tell this stone to become a loaf of bread. ”
The expression suggests that one can begin desiring something by simply looking at it.
In what way did Satan use “the desire of the eyes ” to tempt Jesus, and how did Jesus respond?
What about in Jesus ’ case?
Satan “showed all the kingdoms of the inhabited earth in an instant of time; and the Devil said to him: ‘ I will give you all this authority and the glory of them. ’ ” Jesus would not with his literal eyes see all the kingdoms in an instant, but Satan must have felt that the glory of these kingdoms, as presented in a vision, would hold some attraction for Jesus.
Shamelessly, Satan suggested: “If you do an act of worship before me, it will all be yours. ” In no way did Jesus want to be the kind of person Satan wanted him to be.
But they did manifest pride. When tempting Eve, Satan insinuated that God withheld something wonderful from her.
Satan thus suggested that Eve could gain independence from Jehovah. Pride was apparently a factor that caused her to accept the lie.
She partook of the forbidden fruit, believing that she would not really die. How wrong she was!
What was another way that Satan tried to tempt Jesus, and how did Jesus respond? In contrast with Eve, what a wonderful example of humility Jesus set!
Satan tried to tempt him in another way, but Jesus rejected even the very thought of doing something dramatic that would put God to the test. That would have been an act of pride!
Will such enticements work on us? How can we imitate Jesus in resisting Satan’s temptations?
If we are diligent students of the Bible, we will be well - acquainted with the Scriptures and be able to bring to mind the verses that can help us to keep our thinking straight in the face of temptation.
Stressing the need to have tight control over what we allow ourselves to think about, Paul stated: “We are overturning reasonings and every lofty thing raised up against the knowledge of God; and we are bringing every thought into captivity to make it obedient to the Christ. ”
Our thoughts have a powerful effect on the kind of person we are, so we need to “continue considering ” upbuilding things. — Phil.
We cannot expect to be holy if we are feeding on improper thoughts and desires. We must love Jehovah with “a clean heart. ”
But the heart is treacherous, and we may not even realize how deeply “the things in the world ” are affecting us.
Therefore, should we not ‘ keep testing whether we are in the faith, keep proving what we ourselves are ’ by honest self - examination in light of what we study in the Bible? — 2 Cor.
Why should we be determined to be the kind of person Jehovah wants us to be?
Another factor that helps us to resist “the things in the world ” is that we keep in mind John’s inspired words:“ The world is passing away and so is its desire, but he that does the will of God remains forever. ”
Remembering that fact will help us not to be seduced by the Devil’s enticements. The apostle Peter exhorts us to be the sort of person God approves of as we are “awaiting and keeping close in mind the presence of the day of Jehovah, through which the heavens being on fire will be dissolved and the elements being intensely hot will melt! ”
Despite an uncertain health condition that might have seemed unbearable, I have felt the loving support of our dear heavenly Father throughout my life. And for more than 20 years, I have found special joy serving Jehovah as a pioneer.
