As Jews under the Mosaic Law, Jesus and his apostles shared in the annual Passover.
The last time they did so, Jesus instituted a new event that his followers thereafter were to keep annually — the Lord’s Evening Meal.
In considering the Passover and the Lord’s Evening Meal, what question arises?
Given that Jesus instituted the Lord’s Evening Meal right after the last valid Passover, this new event would coincide with the day of the Passover. You may have observed, though, that the date of the Jewish Passover shown on some modern calendars may differ one or more days from the date when we commemorate Christ’s death.
The answer, in part, involves God’s command to the Israelites. After saying that “the whole congregation of the assembly of Israel must slaughter ” the lamb, Moses specified when on Nisan 14 they were to do so. — Read Exodus 12: 5, 6.
So the lamb was to be slaughtered after the sun had set but while there was still light, at the start of Nisan 14.
So Exodus 12: 6 was understood to refer to the end of Nisan 14, between the time when the sun started to decline (after noon) and the end of the day at sunset.
