Most important, after you meditate on what you have read, strive to apply it. Let us consider how we can use the accounts of King Saul and the apostle Peter to help us maintain a spirit of self - sacrifice.
Saul began his kingship with a modest and humble view of himself. He refused to punish Israelites who spoke out against his kingship, even though he could have felt justified in defending his God - given position.
King Saul accepted the guidance of God’s spirit by leading Israel in a successful battle against the Ammonites. Afterward, he humbly gave Jehovah the credit for the victory. — 1 Sam.
When he defeated the Amalekites in battle, he put his own desires ahead of obedience to Jehovah.
And Saul arrogantly built a monument to himself. When the prophet Samuel told him that Jehovah was displeased, Saul tried to justify himself by focusing on the part of God’s command that he had obeyed and by blaming others for his error.
Additionally, pride made Saul more concerned with saving face before the people than with pleasing God.
How can we use the account about Saul as a mirror to help us maintain the spirit of self - sacrifice? 10, 11. (a) What does Saul’s experience teach us about maintaining a self - sacrificing spirit?
This would be like using a mirror to admire our new clothes while not noticing some dirt on our face.
