We will consider how to (1) ask questions that draw the person out, (2) reason on what the Scriptures say, and (3) use illustrations to drive home our point.
Why is it important to use questions to help us to determine what a person believes?
Questions can help us to determine what a person believes.
Indeed, before delving into a discussion of the Bible’s viewpoint on a certain topic, we do well to try to determine what our listener really believes.
How can questions help us learn why a person believes as he does?
They may find it hard to reconcile such suffering with the existence of a loving Creator. Therefore, if a householder expresses doubts about God’s existence, we could ask, “Have you always felt that way? ”
If the person says no, we might ask whether something in particular has caused him to doubt that God exists.
What should we do after asking a question?
Before offering proof that God exists, we do well to sympathize with the person and let him know that it is not wrong to wonder why we suffer. Our patient and loving approach may move him to want to learn more.
On what does much of our effectiveness in the ministry depend? Let us now consider how to reason on what the Scriptures say.
The Bible is, of course, our primary tool in the ministry. It enables us to be “fully competent, completely equipped for every good work. ”
Much of our effectiveness in the ministry depends, not on the number of scriptures we read, but on the way we reason on and explain those we do read.
Scenario 1: In our ministry, we meet someone who believes that Jesus is equal to God. What scriptures could we use to reason on the matter?
Would that One not be greater than Jesus?
After all, the sender is superior to the one who is sent. ”
His belief in hellfire may be rooted in a desire to see wicked people pay a penalty for their bad deeds. How might we reason with an individual who feels that way?
We could then ask, “If Adam and Eve were in danger of being tormented forever, would it not have been fair to warn them of that? ” We could then read Genesis 3: 19, where sentence was pronounced after their sin but nothing was said about hellfire.
Instead, Adam was told that he would return to the dust.
We could ask the person if he agrees that in order for something to be no more, it had to exist in the first place.
Likely, he will say yes. Then we could point out that there has never been death in heaven; people die only here on earth.
Why did Jesus use illustrations?
How can we use illustrations in our own teaching? How might we illustrate that God is superior to Jesus?
For example, when explaining that God is superior to Jesus, perhaps we can try the following approach. We can mention that both God and Jesus used a family reference when describing their relationship.
Next, we might ask the householder: “If you wanted to teach me that two people are equal, what type of family relationship would you use to illustrate the point? ”
If he does, we could point out how natural that comparison seems to be. Then we might ask: “If you and I could come up with this illustration so readily, would not Jesus — the Great Teacher — have thought of the same comparison?
Jesus thus portrayed God as being older and having more authority than he had. ”
What illustration shows that it would be illogical for God to have the Devil torment people in hellfire? Consider another example.
An illustration may help a parent to see how illogical it would be for God to have the Devil torment people in hellfire. We could say something like this: “Imagine that your child has become very rebellious and is doing many bad things.
At this point, we might ask the parent what he would do if the child rejected all efforts to help him.
Most parents would say that eventually they would have no choice but to punish the child. We could then ask, “What if you found out that an evil person had influenced your child to become so rebellious? ”
Driving home the point of the illustration, we might ask the parent, “Knowing that an evil individual had influenced your child, would you ask that person to punish your child for you? ”
We realize that not everyone we preach to will accept the Kingdom message.
That would be true even if we were to ask just the right questions, use the best reasoning, and come up with the finest illustrations. After all, relatively few responded to Jesus ’ teaching — and he was the greatest Teacher ever to walk the earth! — John 6: 66; 7: 45 - 48.
May we take full advantage of the training Jehovah provides, confident that it will benefit both us and those who listen to us.
