What is the Golden Rule, and how can we apply it?
What is the Golden Rule?
It is the counsel Jesus gave when he said: “All things... that you want men to do to you, you also must do to them. ” How can we apply that rule?
Basically, by taking two steps.
First, we should ask ourselves, ‘ If I were in the other person’s shoes, how would I want to be treated? ’
Then, we need to follow through, accommodating the other person to the extent possible. — 1 Cor.
But Jesus did not indicate that we should extend this consideration only to those related to us in the faith.
Where am I approaching them?
When is the best time to approach people?
How should I approach them?
As we shall see, these questions can help us consider the feelings of those to whom we preach and adapt our approach accordingly. — 1 Cor.
In our ministry, we usually speak to individuals.
Each person has his or her unique background and problems.
When trying to share the good news with someone, ask yourself: ‘ If our roles were reversed, how would I want this person to view me?
Would I be pleased if he viewed me only as a nondescript resident of the neighborhood?
Or would I prefer that he get to know me as a person? ’
Considering such questions can remind us to treat each householder as an individual.
What should we do if someone we meet in the ministry seems to be belligerent?
No one likes to be labeled “a negative person. ” To illustrate: As Christians, we do our best to apply the Bible’s counsel to ‘ let our words always be gracious. ’
Being imperfect, however, we sometimes say things that we later regret.
If we were to speak unkindly to someone — perhaps because we were having a bad day — we would not want to be labeled “rude ” or“ inconsiderate. ”
If someone you meet in the ministry seems to be belligerent, would it not be good to give him the benefit of the doubt?
Could it be that he is under pressure at work or at school?
Is he dealing with a serious health problem?
