First make your peace with your brother, and then come back and offer your gift. ” God will bless us when we show love for our brother and take prompt action to make peace with him.
Do not be a faultfinder.
Or how can you say to your brother, ‘ Allow me to remove the straw from your eye, ’ when look! a rafter is in your own eye?
Hypocrite!
First remove the rafter from your own eye, and then you will see clearly how to remove the straw from your brother’s eye. ”
What a powerful way to tell us not to criticize the small faults of others when we ourselves may have very large faults!
In what unique way do we show love for our neighbor?
We want to show love for our neighbor in a unique way. Like Jesus, we preach the good news of the Kingdom.
Jesus commissioned his followers to “make disciples of people of all the nations. ”
By doing our part to fulfill that commission, we try to help our neighbor to leave the broad and spacious road that leads to destruction and get on the cramped road leading off into life. There is no question that Jehovah blesses such efforts.
Like Jesus, we help people to become conscious of their spiritual need.
By preaching the good news, then, we truly are showing neighbor love in a vitally important way. How do you feel about sharing in our activity as Kingdom proclaimers?
Through effective return visits and home Bible studies, we have the satisfaction of helping people to conform to God’s righteous standards.
In the case of many, despair is thus replaced by joy, and undue anxiety by confidence in our heavenly Father. How good it is to observe the spiritual progress made by new ones!
Do you not agree that it is a blessing to demonstrate love for our neighbor in this special way as Kingdom proclaimers? In your own words, mention some aspects of love covered at 1 Corinthians 13: 4 - 8.
In dealing with our neighbor, putting into practice what Paul wrote concerning love can prevent many problems, can result in happiness, and can bring us God’s blessing.
How can we act in harmony with 1 Corinthians 13: 5, 6? Love will cause us to behave in a decent way toward our neighbor.
Genuine love is not easily provoked and “does not keep account of the injury, ” as though we were making entries in a ledger when others do something unloving.
Were we to hold a grudge, we would not be pleasing God and would, in effect, be tending a smoldering fire that could flare up, harming us and others.
Love makes us rejoice with the truth, but it will not allow us to “rejoice over unrighteousness, ” even if a person who hates us is mistreated or suffers some injustice. — Read Proverbs 24: 17, 18. What do we learn about love from 1 Corinthians 13: 7, 8?
What Scriptural counsel should move us to keep on displaying love for our neighbor?
By applying the Bible’s counsel, we can continue to show love for our neighbor. Such love embraces all people, not just those of our own ethnic background.
Both God and Christ expect us to love our neighbor. If we are not sure what to do in a certain situation involving our neighbor, let us pray for God to guide us through holy spirit.
The command to love our neighbor as ourself is called “the royal law. ”
After referring to some commandments of the Mosaic Law, Paul stated: “Whatever other commandment there is, is summed up in this saying: ‘ You must love your neighbor as yourself. ’ Love does not work evil to one’s neighbor; therefore, love is the law’s fulfillment. ”
As we meditate on why we should show love for our neighbor, it is good to reflect on Jesus ’ statement that his Father “makes his sun rise on both the wicked and the good and makes it rain on both the righteous and the unrighteous. ” We need to display love toward our neighbor, whether he is righteous or unrighteous.
How does Jehovah view those who seem to be weaker in the congregation?
What will help us to adopt Jehovah’s view of human weakness?
How does assisting those who feel weak bring blessings to many?
Why could Paul sympathize with the weak?
A bout of flu or allergies may weaken us to the point that we find it difficult to cope with our daily activities. Now imagine that you have felt feeble, not just for one or two weeks, but for months on end.
The apostle Paul knew what it was like to be affected, even weakened, by pressures from outside the congregation and from within it.
