Cameron: Jon, I’ve really enjoyed the regular discussions we’ve been having about the Bible.
Jon: Yes, I was reading one of your publications, and it said that God’s Kingdom started ruling in 1914. That made me curious because you say that you base all of your beliefs on the Bible.
Jon: Well, I’ve read through the Bible myself. But I can’t remember ever seeing a passage that mentioned the year 1914.
Jon: I do. There’s nothing like it.
Second, I want to commend you for turning to the Bible when trying to find an answer to your question. You did exactly what the Bible encourages us to do: “Keep searching ” for understanding.
In fact, I dug around a little more and found some information about 1914 in this book we’ve been studying. It mentions a dream that a king had — it was about a big tree that was cut down and then grew back or something like that.
I read the prophecy over and over. But to be honest, I still don’t see what it has to do with God’s Kingdom or the year 1914.
Cameron: Actually, Jon, even the prophet Daniel didn’t understand the full meaning of what he was inspired to record!
Jon: I’m not the only one then. That makes me feel a little better.
Jon: Why do you say that?
Daniel 12: 9 says: “The words are to be kept secret and sealed up until the time of the end. ”
Jon: So, can you explain the prophecy in Daniel to me?
Cameron: I’ll do my best.
Cameron: To begin, let me briefly summarize what King Nebuchadnezzar saw in his dream. Then we can talk about what it means.
According to Daniel 4: 17, the prophecy was given “so that people living may know that the Most High is Ruler in the kingdom of mankind and that he gives it to whomever he wants. ”
Jon: Yes, it says that “the Most High is Ruler in the kingdom of mankind. ”
Jon: I guess that’s talking about God. Cameron: Correct.
Jon: What do you mean?
Jon: OK. It says: “In the days of those kings the God of heaven will set up a kingdom that will never be destroyed.
And this kingdom will not be passed on to any other people. It will crush and put an end to all these kingdoms, and it alone will stand forever. ”
Cameron: Thank you. Would you say that this verse sounds as if it is referring to God’s Kingdom?
Cameron: Well, notice that it says that this Kingdom “will stand forever. ” That’s true of God’s Kingdom, but it’s not something that we can say of any human government, can we?
Jon: It mentions a kingdom. Cameron: That’s right.
Notice it says that this Kingdom would have authority over “peoples, nations, and language groups. ” In other words, this Kingdom would have global rulership.
It does say that. Cameron: Also, notice what else the prophecy says: “His rulership is an everlasting rulership that will not pass away, and his kingdom will not be destroyed. ”
His rulership was interrupted when the tree was chopped down and left for seven times — that is, when he lost his sanity for a period of time.
Jon: What do you mean?
So the rulership of those kings was really an expression of God’s rulership. In time, however, most of those kings became disobedient to God and most of their subjects followed suit.
So the big question is: When did the seven times end? If we can answer that question, we will know when God’s Kingdom began ruling.
You got it. Jon: But how do we know that?
And major world events since then confirm that 1914 was indeed the year that God’s Kingdom began ruling in heaven. It was the year when this world entered its last days, or the time of the end.
Jon: Yes, I’m wondering about that.
Cameron: The Bible itself helps us to determine the precise length of the seven times. Would you like to examine that topic the next time I’m here?
Jon: That sounds good.
Do you have a particular Bible subject that you have wondered about?
If so, do not hesitate to ask one of Jehovah’s Witnesses.
